"Internal Error ()\nciteturn9551view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1805.05306v3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://math.dartmouth.edu/~pw/papers/sort.pdf)\nciteturn9551view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 13; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://math.dartmouth.edu/~pw/papers/sort.pdf\",\"lineno\":64}); Total lines: 660\nL0@P0: Sorting by Placement and Shift\nL1@P0: Sergi Elizalde ∗ Peter Winkler †\nL2@P0: June 26, 2008\nL3@P0: Abstract\nL4@P0: In sorting situations where the final destination of each item is known, it is natural to\nL5@P0: repeatedly choose items and place them where they belong, allowing the intervening items\nL6@P0: to shift by one to make room. (In fact, a special case of this algorithm is commonly used\nL7@P0: to hand-sort files.) However, it is not obvious that this algorithm necessarily terminates.\nL8@P0: We show that in fact the algorithm terminates after at most 2n−1 −1 steps in the worst\nL9@P0: case (confirming a conjecture of L. Larson), and that there are super-exponentially many\nL10@P0: permutations for which this exact bound can be achieved. The proof involves a curious\nL11@P0: symmetrical binary representation.\nL12@P0: 1 The Problem\nL13@P0: Suppose that a permutation π ∈ Sn is fixed and represented by the sequence π(1), . . . , π(n).\nL14@P0: Any number i with π(i) 6= i may be “placed” in its proper position, with the numbers in\nL15@P0: positions between i and π(i) shifted up or down as necessary. Repeatedly placing numbers\nL16@P0: until the identity permutation is achieved constitutes a process we call homing. One might\nL17@P0: imagine that the numbers are written on billiard balls in a trough, as in Figure 1 below, where\nL18@P0: the shift is a natural result of moving a ball to a new position.\nL19@P0: 3 4 5 6 7 1 2 8\nL20@P0: 2 1\nL21@P0: 3\nL22@P0: 6 7 8 5 4\nL23@P0: 1 2\nL24@P0: 3\nL25@P0: 6 7 8 5 4\nL26@P0: Figure 1: Two steps in homing\nL27@P0: ∗Department of Mathematics, Dartmouth, Hanover NH 03755-3551, USA; sergi.elizalde@dartmouth.edu.\nL28@P0: †Department of Mathematics, Dartmouth, Hanover NH 03755-3551, USA; peter.winkler@dartmouth.edu.\nL29@P0-1: 1To be precise, if π(i) > i and i is placed, the resulting permutation π\nL30@P1: ′\nL31@P1: is given by\nL32@P1: π\nL33@P1: ′\nL34@P1: (j) =\nL35@P1: \nL36@P1: \nL37@P1: \nL38@P1: π(j) if π(j) < i or π(j) > π(i)\nL39@P1: i if j = i\nL40@P1: π(j)−1 if i < π(j) < π(i)\nL41@P1: and if π(i) < i, we have\nL42@P1: π\nL43@P1: ′\nL44@P1: (j) =\nL45@P1: \nL46@P1: \nL47@P1: \nL48@P1: π(j) if π(j) < π(i) or π(j) > i\nL49@P1: i if j = i\nL50@P1: π(j)+1 if π(i) < j < i\nL51@P1: The primary question we answer is: how many steps does homing take in the worst case?\nL52@P1: 2 History\nL53@P1: Despite its simplicity, homing seems not to have been considered before in the literature; it\nL54@P1: arose recently as a result of a misunderstanding (details below). It is, of course, only in a\nL55@P1: loose sense a sorting algorithm at all, since it requires that the final position of each item be\nL56@P1: known, and presumes that it is desirable to sort “in place.” Thus, it makes sense primarily\nL57@P1: for physical objects. Nonetheless, one can imagine a situation where a huge linked list is to\nL58@P1: be sorted in response to on-line information about where items ultimately belong; then it may\nL59@P1: seem reasonable to place items as information is received, allowing the items between to shift\nL60@P1: up or down by one. We do not recommend this procedure!\nL61@P1: In hand-sorting files, it is common to find the alphabetically first file and move it to the\nL62@P1: front, then find the alphabetically second file and move it to the position behind the first file,\nL63@P1: et cetera. This is a (fast) special case of homing.\nL64@P1: Homing was brought to our attention by mathematician and reporter Barry Cipra [2]. Cipra\nL65@P1: had been looking at John H. Conway’s “Topswops” algorithm [4], in which only the leftmost\nL66@P1: number is placed and intervening numbers are reversed. Topswops terminates when the 1 is\nL67@P1: in position, even if the rest of the numbers are still scrambled. Seeking to get everything in\nL68@P1: order, Cipra considered allowing any not-at-home number to be placed, again reversing the in\u0002tervening numbers. This algorithm does not necessarily terminate, however; a cycling example\nL69@P1: (71325684 → 71348652 → 56843172 → 52713486 → 52317486 → 71325486 → 71325684) was\nL70@P1: provided to Cipra by David Callan, of the University of Wisconsin [1].\nL71@P1: When Cipra tried to explain his interest to his friend Loren Larson (co-author of The\nL72@P1: Inquisitive Problem Solver [7]) the latter thought that the intervening items were to be shifted.\nL73@P1: Cipra liked the new procedure, especially as he was able to show it did always terminate, and\nL74@P1: designed a game around it. The game involved sorting vertical strips of famous paintings (such\nL75@P1: as Picasso’s Guernica); Cipra called it “PermutARTions.” A prototype of the game, renamed\nL76@P1: “Picture This,” has since been made by puzzle designer Oskar van Deventer.\nL77@P1: The neatest proof known to us that homing always terminates is due to Noam Elkies [3].\nL78@P1: Since there are only finitely many states, non-termination would imply the existence of a cycle;\nL79@P1: let k be the largest number which is placed upward in the cycle. (If no number is placed\nL80@P1: upward, the lowest number placed downward is used in a symmetric argument). Once k is\nL81@P1: placed, it can be dislodged upward and placed again downward, but nothing can ever push it\nL82@P1: below position k. Hence it can never again be placed upward, a contradiction.\nL83@P1-2: 23 Outline\nL84@P2: In Section 4 we will consider fast homing, that is, the minimum number of steps needed to sort\nL85@P2: from a given permutation π. Among other things we will see that homing can always be done\nL86@P2: in at most n−1 steps (with a single worst-case example), and that there is an easy sequence\nL87@P2: of choices which respects that bound.\nL88@P2: In Section 5 we prove that homing cannot take more than 2n−1 − 1 steps; in Section 6, we\nL89@P2: show that there are super-exponentially many permutations which can support exactly 2n−1−1\nL90@P2: steps.\nL91@P2: Finally, in Section 7, we wrap up and conclude with some open questions.\nL92@P2: 4 Fast Homing\nL93@P2: A placement of either the least or the greatest number not currently in its home will be called\nL94@P2: extremal; such a number i will never subsequently be dislodged from its home, since no other\nL95@P2: number will ever cross i on its way. Hence,\nL96@P2: Theorem 4.1. Any algorithm that always places the smallest or largest available number will\nL97@P2: terminate in at most n−1 steps.\nL98@P2: Proof. After n−1 numbers are home, the nth must be as well.\nL99@P2: The algorithm which places the smallest not-at-home number is the one cited above, often\nL100@P2: used to hand-sort files. The precise number of steps required is the smallest j such that the\nL101@P2: files which belong in positions j+1, j+2, . . . , n are already in the correct order.\nL102@P2: Suppose placements are random, that is, at each step a uniformly random number is chosen\nL103@P2: from among those that are not at home and then placed. Let us say that a permutation is\nL104@P2: in “stage k” if k (but not k+1) of the extremal numbers are home; thus, e.g., 1,2,3,7,4,6,5,8,9\nL105@P2: is in stage 5, since 1, 2, 3, 8 and 9 are home. There is no stage n−1. If π is in stage k, for\nL106@P2: k < n, then with probability at least 2/(n−k), the next placement will leave it in stage k+1\nL107@P2: or higher. It follows that the expected number of placements needed to move up from stage k\nL108@P2: is at most (n−k)/2, and thus the total expected number of random placements needed to sort\nL109@P2: a permutation cannot exceed Pn−2\nL110@P2: k=0\nL111@P2: 1\nL112@P2: 2\nL113@P2: (n−k). We conclude:\nL114@P2: Theorem 4.2. The expected number of steps required by random homing from π ∈ Sn is at\nL115@P2: most 1\nL116@P2: 4\nL117@P2: (n(n+1) − 2).\nL118@P2: We now return our attention to well-chosen steps, seeking a lower bound.\nL119@P2: Theorem 4.3. Let k be the length of the longest increasing subsequence in π. Then no sequence\nL120@P2: of fewer than n−k placements can sort π.\nL121@P2: Proof. Otherwise there are k+1 numbers which are never placed, and thus remain in their orig\u0002inal order; but that order cannot be correct, else it would constitute an increasing subsequence\nL122@P2: of length k+1 in π.\nL123@P2: Corollary 4.4. The reverse permutation n, n−1, . . . , 1 requires n−1 steps.\nL124@P2: Since [6, 8] the mean length of the longest increasing subsequence of a random π ∈ Sn is\nL125@P2: asymptotically 2√n, we can deduce from Theorem 4.3 that a random permutation requires,\nL126@P2: on average, at least n − 2\nL127@P2: √\nL128@P2: n steps to sort.\nL129@P2-3: 3In general, n minus the length of the longest increasing subsequence is not enough steps to\nL130@P3: sort π. An example (the only example for n = 5) is provided by the permutation 41352, which\nL131@P3: cannot be sorted using only two placements.\nL132@P3: Theorem 4.5. The reverse permutation is the only case requiring n−1 steps.\nL133@P3: Proof. By induction on n, the n = 1 case being trivial. If π is not the reverse permutation,\nL134@P3: there must be i < j with π\nL135@P3: −1\nL136@P3: (i) < π−1(j). Moreover, for n > 2 it cannot be that the only such\nL137@P3: pair is i = 1, j = n. Hence either 1 or n can be placed still leaving a non-reverse permutation\nL138@P3: of the remaining numbers, which can be sorted in n−2 steps by the induction hypothesis.\nL139@P3: Existence of a unique worst case (especially this one) for a sorting algorithm is hardly\nL140@P3: surprising. When we instead maximize the number of steps, something startlingly different\nL141@P3: takes place.\nL142@P3: 5 Slow Homing\nL143@P3: How long can homing take? It is not hard to verify that if one begins with the permutation\nL144@P3: 2, 3, . . . , n−1, n, 1 and always places the left-most not-at-home entry, the result is 2n−1−1 steps\nL145@P3: before the identity permutation is reached (via the familiar “tower of Hanoi” pattern). Larson\nL146@P3: [5] conjectured that 2n−1 − 1 is the maximum. Indeed, although many other, more complex,\nL147@P3: permutations can also support 2n−1 − 1 steps, none permit more.\nL148@P3: Theorem 5.1. Homing always terminates in at most 2\nL149@P3: n−1 − 1 steps.\nL150@P3: To prove Theorem 5.1 we will require several lemmas and some backward analysis. The\nL151@P3: reverse of homing, which we will call evicting, entails choosing a number which is where it\nL152@P3: belongs and displacing it, that is, putting it somewhere else, again shifting the intervening\nL153@P3: values up or down by one. Our objective is then to show that beginning with the identity\nL154@P3: permutation on {1, 2, . . . , n}, at most 2n−1 − 1 displacements are possible. This is trivial for\nL155@P3: n = 1 and we will proceed by induction on n.\nL156@P3: Lemma 5.2. After 2\nL157@P3: n−2 displacements, both 1 and n have been displaced and will never be\nL158@P3: displaced again.\nL159@P3: Proof. Let us observe first that the numbers 1 and n can each be displaced only once, since\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://math.dartmouth.edu/~pw/papers/sort.pdf)\nciteturn9551view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 13; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://math.dartmouth.edu/~pw/papers/sort.pdf\",\"lineno\":235}); Total lines: 660\nL146@P3: [5] conjectured that 2n−1 − 1 is the maximum. Indeed, although many other, more complex,\nL153@P3: values up or down by one. Our objective is then to show that beginning with the identity\nL154@P3: permutation on {1, 2, . . . , n}, at most 2n−1 − 1 displacements are possible. This is trivial for\nL155@P3: n = 1 and we will proceed by induction on n.\nL156@P3: Lemma 5.2. After 2\nL157@P3: n−2 displacements, both 1 and n have been displaced and will never be\nL158@P3: displaced again.\nL159@P3: Proof. Let us observe first that the numbers 1 and n can each be displaced only once, since\nL160@P3: neither can subsequently be shifted back to its proper end. (Equivalently, in the forward\nL161@P3: direction, each can be placed only once.)\nL162@P3: If after 2n−2 displacements one of these values (say, the number 1) has never been displaced,\nL163@P3: then it remains where it began and played no role whatever in the process. Hence the remain\u0002ing n−1 numbers allowed more than 2(n−1)−1 − 1 displacements, contradicting the induction\nL164@P3: hypothesis.\nL165@P3: We now endeavor to show that at most 2n−2 −1 displacements can take place in the second\nL166@P3: stage, after 1 and n have been displaced. To do this we associate with each intermediate state\nL167@P3: π a code α(π), and with each code α, a weight w = w(α).\nL168@P3: The code is a sequence α = (a2, a3, . . . , an−1) of length |α| = n−2 from the alphabet\nL169@P3: {+, −, 0}. Given a permutation π, recall from above that π(i) represents the value in the ith\nL170@P3: position from the left, and therefore π\nL171@P3: −1\nL172@P3: (i) represents the position of the number i. Define\nL173@P3: α(π) by putting ai = + if π\nL174@P3: −1\nL175@P3: (i) > i, that is, if the number i is to the right of where it belongs.\nL176@P3-4: 4Similarly, ai = − if π\nL177@P4: −1\nL178@P4: (i) < i, and ai = 0 if π\nL179@P4: −1\nL180@P4: (i) = i. Thus, a number i can be displaced if\nL181@P4: and only if ai = 0.\nL182@P4: Figure 2 shows an example of a permutation and its code.\nL183@P4: 3 4 5 6 7 1 2 8\nL184@P4: Figure 2: A permutation and its code\nL185@P4: The weight w(α) is defined for codes of all lengths by recursion. If ai = 0 for each i, we\nL186@P4: put w(α) = 0.\nL187@P4: For each i such that ai = −, let di = i−2; for each i with ai = +, let di = n−1−i. Thus,\nL188@P4: di represents the number of symbols to the left of a − or to the right of a +.\nL189@P4: Let i be the index maximizing di; if there are two such values (necessarily one representing\nL190@P4: a − and the other a +), let i be the one for which ai = −. (We will see that this choice has\nL191@P4: no effect.) Let α[i] the code of length |α|−1 obtained by deleting the ith entry of α. Then\nL192@P4: w(α) = w(α[i]) + 2di.\nL193@P4: If the code consists only of 0’s and +’s, then changing the +’s to 1’s gives the binary\nL194@P4: representation of w(α). If instead there are no +’s in the code, then changing every − to a 1\nL195@P4: gives the reverse binary representation of w(α). Thus the code is a sort of double-ended binary\nL196@P4: representation of w(α). Figure 3 shows the recursive derivation of w(α) from a sample code c;\nL197@P4: the gray arrows point to the entry next to be stripped.\nL198@P4: w( )\nL199@P4: =\nL200@P4: + 32\nL201@P4: 8\nL202@P4: 4\nL203@P4: 2\nL204@P4: 1\nL205@P4: 47\nL206@P4: w(\nL207@P4: w(\nL208@P4: w(\nL209@P4: w(\nL210@P4: w(\nL211@P4: )\nL212@P4: )\nL213@P4: )\nL214@P4: )\nL215@P4: )\nL216@P4: =\nL217@P4: + +\nL218@P4: =\nL219@P4: 32\nL220@P4: + 8 + +\nL221@P4: =\nL222@P4: 32\nL223@P4: + 4 + 8 + +\nL224@P4: =\nL225@P4: 32\nL226@P4: + 2 + 4 + 8 + +\nL227@P4: =\nL228@P4: 32\nL229@P4: Figure 3: Derivation of the weight of a code\nL230@P4: We next make some elementary observations about codes and their weights.\nL231@P4-5: 5Lemma 5.3. The minimum of w(α) over codes α of length k is 0, for the all-0 code, and the\nL232@P5: maximum is 2\nL233@P5: k − 1, for codes of the form +p−q\nL234@P5: .\nL235@P5: Proof. This follows from the fact that during the recursion, in reducing the length of α from\nL236@P5: k to k−1 the weight change is at most 2k−1, and achieves that value only when a − is deleted\nL237@P5: from the right or a + from the left.\nL238@P5: Lemma 5.4. Let α = β +p γ −qδ where |β| = |δ|, β contains no +, δ contains no −, and γ\nL239@P5: neither begins with + nor ends with −. Then w(α) = w(βγδ) + 2p+|γ|+q+|β| − 2\nL240@P5: |γ|+|β|\nL241@P5: .\nL242@P5: Proof. Immediate from the definition of w(α), since the indicated blocks of +’s and −’s will\nL243@P5: be eliminated before any other entries.\nL244@P5: Corollary 5.5. The definition of the weight of a code does not depend on how ties are broken\nL245@P5: when di = dj .\nL246@P5: Proof. If in the code α di = dj , where ai = + and aj = −, and i < j, then the situation is\nL247@P5: as in Lemma 5.4 and, irrespective of the tiebreak mechanism, the entries of the blocks will be\nL248@P5: taken next and the resulting weight is the same. If i > j (thus all +’s in α lie to the right of\nL249@P5: all −’s), the removal of ai has no effect on dj and vice-versa, so the two operations trivially\nL250@P5: commute.\nL251@P5: Lemma 5.6. For any codes γ and δ, where γ has no +, w(γδ0) ≤ w(γδ) + 2|δ| − 1.\nL252@P5: Proof. Clearly the presence of an extra 0 at the end increments di by 1 whenever ai = +, so if\nL253@P5: a + is stripped from both γδ0 and from γδ the weight change is doubled for the former. In the\nL254@P5: extreme case, if δ = +|δ|, the difference w(γδ0) − w(γδ) is therefore exactly P|δ|−1\nL255@P5: j=0 = 2|δ| − 1.\nL256@P5: When a − is stripped, the weight change is the same, so it would appear that w(γδ0)−w(γδ)\nL257@P5: would then be smaller. The difficulty is that the incremented weights may cause symbols to\nL258@P5: be stripped in a different order in the two codes.\nL259@P5: To fix that problem we employ Corollary 5.5. In deriving w(γδ) ties are broken in favor of\nL260@P5: − (as in the definition of w); when deriving w(γδ0), in favor of +. This will result in symbols\nL261@P5: being stripped in the same order from the two codes, up to the point where all +’s lie to the\nL262@P5: right of all −’s. After that, difor a given symbol is unaffected by stripping symbols of the\nL263@P5: opposite sign, so the order becomes immaterial.\nL264@P5: Lemma 5.7. Let α be any code, and β = (b2, . . . , bn−1) the result of changing some ai = 0 to\nL265@P5: bi = + or bi = −. Then w(β) > w(α).\nL266@P5: Proof. Suppose bi = −; the other case is symmetric (and uses the reflected form of Lemma 5.6).\nL267@P5: The derivations of w(β) and w(α) are the same until biis stripped. Let β\nL268@P5: ′ and α′ be the\nL269@P5: corresponding codes at that point, right before biis stripped. We can write β\nL270@P5: ′ = γδbiε, where\nL271@P5: γ contains no +, ε no −, and |ε| ≤ |γ|.\nL272@P5: We then have\nL273@P5: w(β\nL274@P5: ′\nL275@P5: ) = w(γδε)+2|γ|+|δ| = w(γδ0\nL276@P5: |ε|\nL277@P5: )+w(ε)+2|γ|+|δ| ≥ w(γδ0\nL278@P5: |ε|+1)−(2|δ|+|ε|−1)+w(ε)+2|γ|+|δ|\nL279@P5: (by Lemma 5.6)\nL280@P5: > w(γδ0\nL281@P5: |ε|+1) + w(ε) = w(γδ0ε) = w(α′\nL282@P5: ).\nL283@P5-6: 6Lemma 5.8. Let π be any permutation of {1, . . . , n} in which π(1) 6= 1 and π(n) 6= n, and\nL284@P6: let π\nL285@P6: ′\nL286@P6: be the result of applying some displacement to π. Let α = α(π) and α\nL287@P6: ′ = α(π′\nL288@P6: ); then\nL289@P6: w(α\nL290@P6: ′\nL291@P6: ) > w(α).\nL292@P6: Proof. A displacement chases a value i away from home, thus causing the 0 in position i of\nL293@P6: the code α to become a + or a −. Assume the latter (the alternative argument is symmetric).\nL294@P6: Since the number i is being moved to the left, other numbers will move right one position or\nL295@P6: stay where they are; thus, the other entries of α can change only from − to 0 or from 0 to +.\nL296@P6: We care only about the former possibility, since by Lemma 5.7, changing a 0 to a + can only\nL297@P6: increase w(α).\nL298@P6: However, any change of a − to a 0 in α must have taken place to the left of the entry ai,\nL299@P6: because a number bigger than i but to its left in π cannot get to its home (to the right of\nL300@P6: position i) when i is displaced. Again by Lemma 5.7, we can assume that all the −’s to the\nL301@P6: left of ai change to 0. Let j be the position of the rightmost − to the left of aiin α (if there\nL302@P6: is no such −, let j = 1). Let 2k be the contribution of the − in position i in the computation\nL303@P6: of w(α\nL304@P6: ′\nL305@P6: ).\nL306@P6: If there are any + entries between aj and ai that are stripped after the − in position i in\nL307@P6: α\nL308@P6: ′\nL309@P6: , then their contribution to w(α\nL310@P6: ′\nL311@P6: ) is less (by a factor of 2) than their contribution to w(α).\nL312@P6: Let ℓ be the number of such +’s. The total contribution of these +’s to w(α\nL313@P6: ′\nL314@P6: ) is at most\nL315@P6: 2\nL316@P6: k−1 + 2k−2 + · · · + 2k−ℓ = 2k−ℓ\nL317@P6: (2ℓ − 1). Thus, the difference between their contribution to\nL318@P6: w(α) and their contribution to w(α\nL319@P6: ′\nL320@P6: ) is at most 2k−ℓ(2ℓ − 1) as well. On the other hand, the\nL321@P6: total contribution to w(α) of the − entries to the left of aiin α is at most 2k−ℓ − 1, since each\nL322@P6: adds a different power of 2 less than 2k−ℓ. We conclude that\nL323@P6: w(α\nL324@P6: ′\nL325@P6: ) ≥ w(α) + 2k − (2k−ℓ − 1) − 2\nL326@P6: k−ℓ\nL327@P6: (2ℓ − 1) > w(α).\nL328@P6: Theorem 5.1 is an easy consequence of Lemmas 5.2 and 5.8. In fact nothing prevents us\nL329@P6: from associating to each π ∈ Sn a code of full length n, and applying the above argument to\nL330@P6: conclude directly that there can be no more than 2n − 1 displacements. However, this falls\nL331@P6: short of the desired result by a factor of 2 (as does an argument based on Elkies’ finiteness\nL332@P6: proof); hence the 2-stage argument above.\nL333@P6: 6 Counting Bad Cases\nL334@P6: The proof of Theorem 5.1 tells us somewhat more about the worst-case structure of eviction,\nL335@P6: that is, about the digraph on Sn which boasts an arc from π to π\nL336@P6: ′ when π′\nL337@P6: is among the longest\u0002lived permutations that can be reached from π by a single displacement. We are particularly\nL338@P6: interested in the set Mn of permutations at maximum distance 2n−1 − 1 from the source (the\nL339@P6: identity permutation), since these are the worst-case starting points for homing. The proof\nL340@P6: shows that each permutation in Mn must have a code of the form +k−n−2−k, but the converse\nL341@P6: does not hold in general.\nL342@P6: Let the height h(π) of a permutation π be the distance to π from the source in the above\nL343@P6: digraph (equivalently, the maximum length of a sequence of placements from π to the identity).\nL344@P6: In the rest of the paper, let τn denote the permutation n, 2, 3, . . . , n−1, 1.\nL345@P6: Lemma 6.1. h(τn) = 2n−2.\nL346@P6-7: 7Proof. The only entries that can be placed in the first step are n and 1. By symmetry, we can\nL347@P7: assume that n is placed first. The steps after that are equivalent to homing the permutation\nL348@P7: 2, 3, . . . , n−1, 1 of length n−1. By Theorem 5.1 and the observation above it, we know that\nL349@P7: h(2, 3, . . . , n−1, 1) = 2n−2 − 1.\nL350@P7: Lemma 6.2. For any permutation with code α = +i0\nL351@P7: k−j\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v26i3p25/pdf/)\nciteturn9551view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 19; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v26i3p25/pdf/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1050\n"
